from pathlib import Path
import json,html,textwrap
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
LESSONS=[
(266,'SOLID: five ways to contain change',
'A restaurant separates cooking, billing and record keeping. Changing the payment terminal should not require rewriting the kitchen.',
'SOLID is a set of design principles, not a library and not a promise to eliminate every conditional. The goal is to localize changes while preserving clear contracts. A class can use several principles at once.',
'A checkout initially calculates price, constructs a card SDK, charges and saves receipts in one large method. Adding UPI forces edits throughout it. Separate the workflow from charging and persistence; pass those capabilities into the workflow.',
[('Single Responsibility — SRP','Give a module one coherent reason to change. Pricing changes with pricing policy; receipt persistence changes with storage policy. SRP does not mean one method per class or one class for every line.'),('Open/Closed — OCP','Keep the stable checkout workflow closed to repeated edits by accepting a Charger interface. Add a conforming UPI implementation at the variation point. Registration/configuration may still change; no useful design is closed to every possible change.'),('Liskov Substitution — LSP','Every Charger must honor the documented contract: accepted inputs, result meaning and failure semantics. A subclass that suddenly rejects valid card-independent amounts or silently does nothing breaks substitutability. Inheritance alone does not prove a valid subtype.'),('Interface Segregation — ISP','A payment-only client should depend on Charger rather than a giant interface that also requires settlement reports and refunds. Do not force an implementation to provide unsupported methods by throwing UnsupportedOperationException.'),('Dependency Inversion — DIP','The workflow depends on payment and receipt abstractions. Constructor injection supplies implementations; it does not create a global service locator. Dependency injection is a construction technique; DIP is the direction of dependency.'),('Spring Boot connection','Make the coordinator a @Service with constructor parameters for the capabilities. Register implementations as beans; if multiple match, choose explicitly using @Qualifier or a registry. The runnable lab uses plain constructor injection so it needs no Spring dependencies.')],
[('Define contracts','Document positive amount and receipt/error meaning for every Charger implementation.'),('Inject dependencies','Checkout receives Charger and ReceiptStore; it does not construct provider SDKs.'),('Run one workflow','Validate the amount, delegate charging, then persist the receipt.'),('Substitute implementation','Run the same checks with Card and UPI; a new provider should preserve the contract.')],
'Too many interfaces make a small stable design harder to follow. Extract real variation points. An interface and constructor injection alone do not solve remote payment atomicity or retry safety.',
'If a new Charger rejects an input that the interface says is valid, which SOLID principle has it violated?',
'The lab checks two interchangeable charging implementations, receipt persistence and a rejected zero amount. Charges are simulated; this is not a production payment transaction.'),
(267,'Choose a creation pattern by its purpose',
'Factory Method chooses who makes a product; Abstract Factory chooses a compatible kit; Builder assembles one configured product.',
'All three concern object creation, but they address different problems. Distinguish an actual Factory Method overridden by subclasses from a helper with a switch that is usually called a simple factory.',
'A UI needs web-specific buttons, a matching dark-themed button and checkbox, and an audit report with optional charts. These are three separate creation decisions, not three names for the same factory.',
[('Factory Method','A Dialog base class owns render(), but calls an overridable createButton(). WebDialog chooses the concrete Button. The stable algorithm delegates one creation decision to a subclass. A static create(type) switch can be useful without being this specific pattern.'),('Abstract Factory','UiFactory exposes button() and checkbox(). DarkUi returns a coherent family; another factory can return a light family. Clients work through product interfaces rather than mixing incompatible concrete families.'),('Builder','Report.Builder collects title and optional chart settings, then build() validates and creates the finished immutable value. A builder is useful for many options or multi-stage construction; a record with two obvious fields may be simpler with a constructor.'),('How they differ','Factory Method varies creation through subclassing. Abstract Factory provides several related creation operations through a factory object. Builder assembles a product through explicit steps. They can coexist; an abstract factory implementation might internally use builders.'),('Spring Boot connection','Spring @Bean methods and configuration can perform application assembly without a custom factory hierarchy. Use a builder for request/report values when useful; use container-managed lifecycle for services that require injection or destruction callbacks.')],
[('Factory Method demo','WebDialog overrides createButton(); inherited render() uses the supplied product.'),('Abstract Factory demo','One DarkUi object creates both related dark-themed controls.'),('Builder demo','Set title and includeChart, then call build() to obtain the finished report.'),('Validate construction','Verify created behavior and reject a report whose required title is missing.')],
'A builder should not leak a partially built object or retain mutable references without defensive copies. Abstract factories add types; use them when a family relationship matters, not for every new expression.',
'Would a report with many optional sections need an Abstract Factory, a Builder, or just a constructor—and why?',
'The lab separately exercises a subclass creation hook, a compatible product family, and builder validation.'),
(268,'Strategy: swap behavior behind a contract',
'A navigation app can choose a walking or driving route while the caller still asks for a route.',
'Strategy encapsulates interchangeable behavior behind a stable interface. The context delegates to a selected strategy. Selection policy and the algorithm itself are separate responsibilities.',
'Checkout receives payment method upi and amount 5,000 paise. A validated registry selects the UPI strategy and invokes it with the original purchase key. Selecting card changes behavior without duplicating checkout validation.',
[('Roles','PaymentStrategy is the contract; Card and Upi implement behavior; Checkout is the context; the method-to-strategy map performs selection. Name the roles before discussing frameworks.'),('Why it improves a switch','An expanding switch often mixes selection, validation and provider implementation. A registry keeps selection small while each provider can be tested separately. A short stable switch is still reasonable when no meaningful variation exists.'),('Strategy versus State','Strategy is selected to perform an interchangeable operation. State represents the context’s lifecycle, such as Pending to Paid, and governs allowed transitions. Both may use interfaces, but their intent and ownership differ.'),('Factory versus Strategy','A factory supplies an object. A strategy performs behavior. A factory can create/select a strategy, but creation and execution remain different concerns.'),('Spring Boot connection','Inject provider beans and build an immutable registry with explicit keys. Reject duplicate or unknown keys at an appropriate boundary. Keep provider credentials server-side; do not let an arbitrary client choose an unauthorized implementation.'),('Distributed-systems limit','An interface does not make providers share the same guarantees. Define supported currencies, timeout interpretation, idempotency and result semantics. A timeout can be an unknown charge outcome rather than a clean failure.')],
[('Validate request','Check positive paise and a nonblank purchase key before provider delegation.'),('Select policy','Find exactly the permitted strategy for the requested method; reject unknown methods.'),('Execute strategy','Call selected.pay(amount,key); use the same stable request key for retries.'),('Check outcome','Handle a documented success or failure/unknown outcome; reconcile real provider timeouts.')],
'Too many one-off strategies add indirection. Keep common validation in the context and provider-specific behavior in the implementation. Do not hide provider differences under a misleading universal contract.',
'Which object should decide the selected method, and which object should implement the payment algorithm?',
'The lab uses simulated card/UPI responses, checks strategy selection and rejects an unsupported method. It does not send payments or implement durable deduplication.'),
(269,'Wrap, translate or simplify?',
'A translator changes language, a jacket adds a layer, and a receptionist offers one simple entry point to several departments.',
'Adapter changes an incompatible interface into the interface your code expects. Decorator keeps the same contract while adding behavior. Facade offers a simpler use-case interface over a subsystem. Similar wrapper syntax does not imply the same intent.',
'A legacy document SDK takes an integer ID. The application accepts a string ID, counts attempts and exposes a preview operation. PreviewFacade calls MeteredDocuments, which calls LegacyAdapter, which calls the legacy SDK.',
[('Adapter','Documents.read(String) translates to LegacySdk.fetchNumeric(int). A real adapter also maps identifiers, authorization context, return values and error semantics. Invalid IDs must not silently map to another document.'),('Decorator','MeteredDocuments implements Documents too, so it can wrap any Documents instance. It adds attempt counting and delegates. Choose whether metrics count attempts, success or both. Decorator order matters: retries outside a metrics wrapper count differently from retries inside it.'),('Facade','PreviewFacade exposes preview(id), hiding retrieval and formatting details. It does not need to implement Documents because it offers a different, more convenient use-case boundary. A facade is not automatically a new network service.'),('Spring AOP connection','Proxy-based AOP can apply cross-cutting behavior such as metrics around eligible method calls. That is not identical to every decorator; proxy boundaries and self-invocation affect whether advice runs. Keep access checks and transaction semantics explicit.'),('Migration connection','A stable Documents contract lets a new repository adapter replace the old SDK incrementally. Test missing documents, forbidden access, version selection and timeouts as carefully as successful reads.')],
[('Call the facade','preview(42) expresses the user’s requested use case rather than SDK details.'),('Apply decorator','The metrics wrapper records an attempt and delegates through the same read contract.'),('Translate via adapter','Validate/convert the string ID and call the incompatible legacy method.'),('Return through layers','Return content through the wrapper and let the facade format the preview; preserve errors.')],
'Blind retries around non-idempotent writes can duplicate effects. Too many wrappers obscure stack traces. Document wrapper order and avoid changing authorization/error semantics accidentally.',
'Which wrapper is an Adapter if it changes String IDs to integer IDs, and why is the metrics wrapper different?',
'The lab checks preview composition and verifies that the attempt metric includes an invalid-ID failure. It models SDK calls locally.'),
]
LESSONS += [
(270,'Observer, Pub/Sub and Mediator: different decoupling',
'A doorbell alerts nearby listeners; a postal service routes to distant subscribers; an event coordinator tells collaborators what to do next.',
'Observer is an object-level notification relationship. Broker-based publish/subscribe separates producers from consumers through messaging infrastructure. Mediator centralizes collaboration rules so colleagues do not all call one another directly. These are not interchangeable delivery guarantees.',
'OrderCreated needs email and analytics. A local observer invokes listeners in the current process. Separate services may consume the event through a broker. A checkout mediator can instead explicitly coordinate reserve, record and request-payment steps.',
[('Observer','The subject keeps listeners and notifies them when something changes. This demo uses synchronous callbacks, so a slow listener delays publish and an exception stops later callbacks. Observer itself does not mandate synchronous execution or durable delivery.'),('Broker Pub/Sub','A broker routes publications to subscriptions. For email and analytics to each receive every Kafka event, use separate consumer groups; consumers in one group share partitions rather than all receiving each event. A real design needs retention, retries, offsets and consumer idempotency.'),('Mediator','A coordinator owns the collaboration sequence. Components delegate coordination to it rather than directly knowing every other component. An application mediator can be local; a durable distributed saga orchestrator additionally records recoverable state.'),('Choreography versus orchestration','Consumers reacting to events can form choreography. An orchestrator directs a workflow. Neither is universally better: independent notifications suit events, while tightly ordered business decisions may need explicit coordination.'),('Delivery is not effect','A duplicate event must not create a second email or shipment unintentionally. Consumer-local in-memory deduplication only lasts for that process; durable deduplication must share a transaction or idempotent boundary with the business effect. External sends can still have uncertainty.')],
[('Observe locally','Register email and analytics listeners; publishing invokes local callbacks in this demo.'),('Expect duplicate delivery','Publishing the same event again reaches both listeners again; this is deliberately visible.'),('Deduplicate each effect','The demo email consumer remembers event IDs; analytics deliberately records both deliveries.'),('Coordinate explicitly','The separate mediator demo executes reserve, record order and request payment in a defined local order.')],
'A local event bus is not Kafka, and this lab does not run a broker. The in-memory set is not restart-safe or concurrency-safe. Use a broker only when durability, independent lifecycles or distribution justify its cost.',
'If email and analytics are in the same Kafka consumer group, will each always see every order event?',
'The lab checks synchronous observer delivery, duplicate handling in one consumer and a separate mediator sequence. The broker discussion is conceptual; no broker or distributed transaction is simulated.'),
(271,'Singleton scope does not mean thread safety',
'One shared receptionist serves many people, but must not put the current caller’s private details in a shared notebook slot.',
'The Singleton pattern restricts construction and exposes one instance within its defined runtime scope. A Spring singleton is one managed instance for a bean definition in one container. Neither means one global instance across multiple servers, nor does either make mutable fields automatically safe.',
'A shared OrderService receives two simultaneous requests: 5,000 paise × 2 and 7,000 paise × 3. Request amounts belong in method parameters and locals. A field currentCustomer or lastAmount would let callers overwrite each other.',
[('Classic Java singleton','An enum constant gives controlled instance identity per defining classloader. Other patterns use a static holder or synchronization. Classloader boundaries matter; do not call a JVM object a globally unique distributed coordinator.'),('Spring singleton','The container returns the bean instance for that definition. Two bean definitions of the same class can produce two instances, and two application containers have separate scopes. Manually using new bypasses container management.'),('Concurrency safety','Prefer immutable dependencies and local request state. Shared mutable caches, counters and collections need appropriate synchronization or concurrent structures. Stateless service code still depends on the thread-safety and scope of its collaborators.'),('Dependency injection and testing','Inject collaborators rather than looking up global singletons from inside business methods. Tests can supply substitutes; hidden global state otherwise leaks between tests.'),('Cluster-wide rules','With three application replicas there may be three service instances. Use a database uniqueness rule or an appropriate distributed ownership protocol to protect a global booking, not an in-memory singleton lock.')],
[('Create shared service','Construct a service with an immutable Rules dependency; a container would manage this lifetime in Spring.'),('Handle request A','Compute its total from parameters and locals without writing request state to fields.'),('Handle request B','A second thread independently computes another total through the same service object.'),('Check scope','Enum identity is stable within this classloader; a new service object is still distinct from the shared one.')],
'An AtomicInteger protects one counter, not an entire multi-step workflow or other replicas. Do not cache per-request authentication data in singleton fields. Prototype injection also does not magically create one dependency per method call.',
'Why can a singleton bean’s synchronized method fail to protect the last stock item across three application replicas?',
'The Java 21 lab checks enum identity and two concurrent calculations with independent inputs. It uses no Spring container; Spring scope rules are explained separately.'),
(272,'Repository, Unit of Work and Specification',
'A librarian finds records, a transaction folder collects changes, and a filter describes which records you want.',
'Repository presents domain-oriented access to stored aggregates. Unit of Work tracks changes belonging to a business operation and coordinates writing them. Specification represents a reusable selection rule. They cooperate but do not mean the same thing.',
'An auditor lists Alice’s open audit records. A repository accepts a composed owner-is-Alice AND status-is-OPEN rule. A separate operation changes several related records inside one transaction rather than committing each repository call independently.',
[('Repository','Use methods such as findOpenAuditsFor(owner) or matching(specification) to describe intent. Do not blindly wrap every existing repository in another identical CRUD layer. Domain aggregate boundaries and query requirements should shape the interface.'),('Unit of Work','An ORM persistence context tracks managed entities and changes. A service-level transaction commonly defines the business commit/rollback boundary. Unit of Work and database transaction are related concepts, not synonyms; tracking changes does not by itself guarantee ACID behavior.'),('Specification','Combine predicates such as ownedBy(alice).and(isOpen()). The lab uses java.util.function.Predicate over a list. Spring Data JPA Specification builds database Criteria predicates; filtering a fully loaded table in Java is not a scalable substitute.'),('Spring service boundary','A transaction around a service operation can include several repository calls to the same participating transaction manager. Merely adding @Transactional does not atomically include a remote payment service. Proxy invocation and rollback rules must be understood for the chosen setup.'),('Performance and API boundaries','Avoid N+1: loading parents and lazily accessing each child may issue many queries. Use suitable projections, fetch strategies or batching after measuring SQL. Map persistence entities to API values so lazy loading, internal fields and graph shape do not become the public contract.')],
[('Compose intent','Build owner and status predicates; combine them with AND.'),('Query repository','The demo repository evaluates the composed rule against committed local rows.'),('Stage changes','UnitOfWork.save stages a new value; it does not immediately mutate the committed list.'),('Finish boundary','commit copies staged changes; closing without commit discards them in this teaching model.')],
'The list-based lab is not a real transactional database: no isolation, concurrency, durable recovery or ORM dirty checking is provided. Real rollback and constraints must be tested against the actual persistence stack.',
'Why is a composed Java Predicate over all loaded rows different from a JPA Specification executed in SQL?',
'The lab checks a composed query, an explicit staged commit and discarded uncommitted work. It intentionally does not claim database transaction guarantees.'),
(273,'Strangler Fig: replace one capability at a time',
'Renovate a building room by room while keeping a safe entrance, rather than demolishing it with everyone inside.',
'Strangler Fig gradually moves selected capabilities from a legacy system to a replacement behind a stable entry point. Routing, compatibility, data ownership and rollback are part of the migration; a reverse proxy alone is not a complete modernization plan.',
'A document API keeps GET /documents/{id}. Document 42 is validated in the modern repository while document 7 still belongs to the legacy path. The router selects one owner; clients retain the same external contract.',
[('Choose a migration slice','Start with a bounded capability or cohort such as read-only retrieval for migrated documents. Keep authorization, document version and error semantics compatible. Arbitrary percentage routing is unsafe if the selected backend lacks the requested data.'),('Establish data parity','Backfill documents and metadata, verify checksums/counts, compare ACLs and versions, then reconcile changes during the copy. A matching byte count alone does not prove permission or metadata parity.'),('Cut over ownership','Define one authoritative writer per entity/capability during each phase. Replication or change capture needs ordering and idempotency. Uncoordinated writes to old and new stores create divergence.'),('Validate safely','Canary eligible traffic and compare results/latency/errors. Shadow only suitable side-effect-free reads with privacy and access controls; never duplicate payments or writes merely to compare responses.'),('Rollback and retire','Before rollback after modern writes, restore a valid authoritative view or reconcile data; flipping a route is insufficient. After parity and stability, retire legacy paths, jobs, credentials and data according to retention requirements.')],
[('Keep stable entry point','Clients call one Documents API; routing policy identifies eligible migrated entities.'),('Route one owner','The demo routes document 42 to modern and other IDs to legacy; these are alternatives, not both calls.'),('Validate before expansion','Check content, ACL and version parity plus errors and latency before adding more entities.'),('Rollback deliberately','The lab can revert routing only. A real rollback after writes requires data synchronization and ownership recovery.')],
'Two live paths increase operational complexity. A routing facade can become a bottleneck. Big-bang replacement may be reasonable for a small isolated system, but do not assume gradual routing solves dual writes.',
'Why can flipping the route back lose access to new data written only in the replacement system?',
'The lab checks migrated versus legacy routes and route-only rollback. It models no data backfill, ACL migration, dual writes or network calls.'),
]

def build():
 questions=json.loads((ROOT/'data/questions.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
 out=['# Design Patterns — Java & Distributed Systems\n','Eight complete lessons: read the situation, compare the alternatives, follow the diagram, run the Java lab, then explain the failure case.\n','## Start here\n','A **pattern** is a named way to organize collaboration for a recurring problem. It is not a mandatory framework or a recipe to apply everywhere. **Interface** means a contract; **implementation** supplies behavior; **composition** connects objects by delegation; **inheritance** defines a subtype relationship; **injection** supplies a dependency from outside.\n','## Which problem are you solving?\n','| Need | Principle / pattern |\n|---|---|\n| Contain unrelated changes | SOLID |\n| Defer construction of one product | Factory Method |\n| Create compatible related products | Abstract Factory |\n| Assemble a complex configured value | Builder |\n| Swap an algorithm or provider behavior | Strategy |\n| Translate an incompatible interface | Adapter |\n| Add behavior through the same contract | Decorator |\n| Offer a simpler subsystem entry point | Facade |\n| Notify object listeners | Observer |\n| Decouple event producers and remote consumers | Broker Pub/Sub |\n| Coordinate collaboration explicitly | Mediator |\n| Control instance lifetime / scope | Singleton / Spring scopes |\n| Express data access, changes and filters | Repository / Unit of Work / Specification |\n| Replace legacy capabilities gradually | Strangler Fig |\n', 'Examples use original teaching scenarios and Java 21. Plain-Java labs do not start Spring, a broker, a database, or a payment provider. Framework connections and distributed limitations are explained explicitly.\n']
 for ident,title,analogy,concept,scenario,sections,steps,failure,check,labnote in LESSONS:
  q=next(q for q in questions if q['id']==ident)
  code=(ROOT/f'examples/design-patterns/Pattern{ident}.java').read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig')
  q.update(explanation=concept,why=analogy,example=scenario,tradeoffs=failure,detailStatus='Expanded',learningTitle=title,lessonSections=[{'title':a,'explanation':b} for a,b in sections]+[{'title':'What the lab proves','explanation':labnote}],walkthroughSteps=[{'title':a,'explanation':b} for a,b in steps],knowledgeCheck=check,diagramImage=f'docs/design-patterns/diagrams/pattern-{ident}.svg',guidePath='docs/design-patterns-java-distributed-systems.md',labPath=f'examples/design-patterns/Pattern{ident}.java',labType='Java 21',runCommand=f'javac -d .pattern-classes examples/design-patterns/Pattern{ident}.java; java -cp .pattern-classes Pattern{ident}',code=code)
  q['diagram']=' → '.join(a for a,b in steps)
  esc=html.escape
  svg=[f'<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="960" height="960" viewBox="0 0 960 960" role="img" aria-labelledby="title desc"><title id="title">{esc(title)}</title><desc id="desc">{esc(q["diagram"])}. This is an illustrative pattern walkthrough.</desc>', '<defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6" fill="#38bdf8"/></marker></defs>','<rect width="960" height="960" rx="18" fill="#0f172a"/>',f'<text x="40" y="48" fill="#f8fafc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="25" font-weight="bold">{ident}. {esc(title)}</text>','<text x="40" y="80" fill="#cbd5e1" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16">Follow the numbered roles • these are learning steps, not separate network services</text>']
  for i,(label,detail) in enumerate(steps):
   y=110+i*168
   svg += [f'<rect x="40" y="{y}" width="880" height="142" rx="12" fill="#1e293b" stroke="#38bdf8"/>',f'<text x="60" y="{y+30}" fill="#7dd3fc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="21" font-weight="bold">{i+1}. {esc(label)}</text>']
   for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(detail,92,break_long_words=False,break_on_hyphens=False)):svg.append(f'<text x="60" y="{y+60+j*22}" fill="#f1f5f9" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17">{esc(line)}</text>')
   if i<3:svg.append(f'<path d="M480,{y+144} L480,{y+165}" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>')
  svg.append('<text x="40" y="818" fill="#fbbf24" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="19" font-weight="bold">Boundary / failure to explain</text>')
  for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(failure,101,break_long_words=False)):svg.append(f'<text x="40" y="{848+j*21}" fill="#e2e8f0" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="16">{esc(line)}</text>')
  svg.append('</svg>');(ROOT/q['diagramImage']).write_text('\n'.join(svg),encoding='utf-8')
  out += [f'## {ident}. {q["question"]}\n',f'### {title}\n',analogy+'\n','### What it means\n',concept+'\n','### Worked real-world example\n',scenario+'\n']
  for heading,body in sections:out += [f'### {heading}\n',body+'\n']
  out += [f'![{title}](design-patterns/diagrams/pattern-{ident}.svg)\n','### Follow the flow\n']
  out += [f'{i}. **{a}:** {b}\n' for i,(a,b) in enumerate(steps,1)]
  out += ['### Runnable Java 21 example\n',labnote+'\n',f'[Source](../{q["labPath"]})\n',f'```java\n{code.rstrip()}\n```\n',f'```powershell\n{q["runCommand"].replace("; ",chr(10))}\n```\n','Expected: a PASS line; failed invariants throw AssertionError. The .pattern-classes directory holds generated compilation output.\n','### Trade-offs and mistakes\n',failure+'\n','### Check your understanding\n',check+'\n']
 out += ['## Sources and further reading\n','- [Spring bean scopes: per container and bean definition](https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/factory-scopes.html)\n- [Spring Data JPA transaction boundaries](https://docs.spring.io/spring-data/jpa/reference/jpa/transactions.html)\n- [Azure Architecture Center: Strangler Fig](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/strangler-fig)\n','## Regeneration and verification\n','Edit `scripts/expand_design_patterns.py` for lesson content and `examples/design-patterns/*.java` for labs. Run `python scripts/expand_design_patterns.py`, `python scripts/validate_questions.py`, and `python scripts/validate_pattern_labs.py`. The browser check `node scripts/test_system_design.cjs` covers system design and design patterns together.\n']
 (ROOT/'data/questions.json').write_text(json.dumps(questions,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
 (ROOT/'docs/design-patterns-java-distributed-systems.md').write_text('\n'.join(out),encoding='utf-8')
 print('Expanded eight pattern lessons, diagrams and matching Java 21 labs.')

if __name__=='__main__':build()
